    "message": "feat(models): read per-model reasoning capability from models.dev\n\nReasoning is translated per PROVIDER today, with no per-MODEL knowledge:\nthe encoder receives no model information at all and fires unconditionally.\nThat is how a numeric budget reached a model that only accepts an effort\nenum, and how \"xhigh\" reaches a model whose vocabulary stops at \"high\".\n\nmodels.dev already publishes exactly the missing data, and this project\nalready fetches and disk-caches that index for cost, context limit and\nvision -- then discards every reasoning field. This reads them.\n\nPer model, models.dev carries:\n    \"reasoning\": true|false\n    \"reasoning_options\": [{\"type\":\"toggle\"},\n                          {\"type\":\"effort\",\"values\":[...]},\n                          {\"type\":\"budget_tokens\"}]\nMeasured against the live 4 MB index: 192 providers, 6838 models, 4718\nreasoning-capable, 3435 with reasoning_options (effort 2515, toggle 1081,\nbudget_tokens 587). The effort vocabulary -- none, minimal, low, medium,\nhigh, xhigh, max -- is identical to this project's ReasoningEffort enum,\nand the three option types map one-to-one onto the encoder families that\nalready exist.\n\nAdds:\n- ModelReasoningCapability beside ProviderModelInfo. Three independent\n  control-style booleans rather than one enum, because a model can\n  advertise several at once and callers ask about each separately. Every\n  field's None means \"unknown\", deliberately distinct from a known False:\n  the consumer must only change behaviour for capabilities it can confirm.\n- Parsing of reasoning/reasoning_options, tolerating malformed entries and\n  dropping value strings that are not ReasoningEffort members (the stray\n  \"default\") instead of raising.\n- A lookup layered provider-reported -> models.dev -> None.\n\nThe lookup reads the DISK cache, never the in-memory ProviderModelCache.\nThat cache is populated only by the admin refresh endpoint and is empty on\na fresh server, so a lookup gated on it would leave this feature silently\nnever firing -- the failure mode this project has hit four times. There is\na test named for that specific trap.\n\nDeliberately no behaviour change: nothing calls the new lookup yet. No\nencoder, request builder, or routing file is touched. The next PR consumes\nit, and can be reviewed as the behaviour change it actually is.\n\nVerification:\n- 39 tests pass, 20 new: option parsing, unknown-value tolerance,\n  known-false vs unknown, all 18 aliases, absent provider, the layering in\n  all three directions, malformed input, and the empty-cache trap.\n- Mutation-tested: dropping an alias killed 3 tests by name; returning a\n  default record instead of None for an absent model killed 4; gating the\n  lookup on an empty in-memory cache killed the trap test.\n- Verified against the REAL 4 MB models.dev index, not only the fixture:\n  all 18 aliases resolve to providers that exist, known-false is distinct\n  from unknown, and a memoised lookup costs ~21 microseconds.\n- Full suite 4455 passed; 14 pre-existing baseline failures.\n- ruff format, ruff check, ty check: all clean repo-wide.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
